    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required emitting an AUDIT log line on successful owner creation containing the owner id, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`. Both `customerCode` and `registrationDate` were already computed and populated on the `Owner` before the response was built, so the only missing behavior was the audit log itself.\n\nI added a **single statement** to `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, right after `saveOwner` (once the id is assigned):\n\n```java\norg.slf4j.LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\").info(\"owner id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n    owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n```\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** `addOwner` has cyclomatic complexity 1 (no branches), and this is a single added line, so its per-function cost stays `WMC_other \u00d7 1 \u00d7 1`. I deliberately inlined the logger rather than introducing a new helper class or a logger field \u2014 a new class would have touched a second file and *doubled* the whole cost via the file multiplier, while the tiny WMC_other savings on a CC-1 method couldn't offset that. No other function's complexity changed, and only one file was touched.\n\nThe test `AcceptanceTest.coreLogsAuditOnCreate` passes, and the full suite builds green.",
